From c74612e261c07936f129147598d517a83977a007 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Zenium Port <port@zenium.invalid>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 18:22:38 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 88/88] soc/mediatek: provide the CMDQ backup-slot pool the landed display core calls, and nothing beyond it

nm -u over the 15 landed display objects lists exactly four cmdq names: cmdqRecWrite (29 link references
from ddp_mutex.o and ddp_rsz.o, via the DISP_REG_* macros) and cmdqBackup{Allocate,Read,Write}Slot
(ddp_drv.o, at ddp_drv.c:95/98/108). This patch supplies the last three. cmdqRecWrite is NOT provided here
and stays out until the GCE provider/binding question is settled from stock evidence: it needs a struct
cmdq_pkt, which needs a cmdq_client, which needs a mailbox channel, and nothing binds the gce node here
(drivers/mailbox/mtk-cmdq-mailbox.c:673 matches only mt8173/mt8183/mt6779/mt8192/mt8195-gce; the landed
node is "mediatek,gce" + "syscon" with no #mbox-cells). No compatible string, #mbox-cells or port-local
provider is added by this patch, deliberately.

What the slot trio really is, measured in the vendor engine rather than assumed from its name:
cmdqBackupAllocateSlot is cmdq_alloc_mem (v3/cmdq_record.c:3948) which is
cmdqCoreAllocWriteAddress(count, &pa, CMDQ_CLT_DISP) (v3/cmdq_helper_ext.c:1996) - a kernel buffer whose BUS
ADDRESS is the handle - and the CPU read and write (cmdq_helper_ext.c:2072, :2159) find the buffer again by
walking a global list under a spinlock and dereferencing plain memory. No GCE register is touched and no
mailbox channel is requested, which is exactly why this half can land on its own. Nothing here claims the
command queue can read what is stored: whether the GCE reaches this memory directly or only through
M4U/SMI translation stays open and is recorded as such.

Verified, not just compiled:
  * gate off (the default any user builds): make vmlinux rc=0, 0 error lines, 0 undefined references, and
    the object is not built at all (nm vmlinux shows no cmdqBackup symbol). vmlinux is byte-for-byte the
    same size as the 0087 tip, 167,987,640 B, because nothing enters the default build.
  * gate on (MTK_DISP_BRINGUP=y, which selects nothing here - the Makefile keys mtk-cmdq-disp-slot.o on the
    same switch): 0 errors, 0 warnings from these two files, object 57,256 B.
  * whole-tree link with the display objects on: undefined references 507 -> 502. The five that go are
    exactly this patch's three symbols (the reads in disp_get_ovl_bandwidth() account for more than one
    reference per name), and cmdqRecWrite remains at 29, as intended.
  * each symbol defined exactly once across every object in the tree (checked with nm over drivers/**).
  * the .config of record does not move at all: d780d6d3d391 before and after, because this patch adds no
    Kconfig symbol.
  * the address arithmetic is tested on the host against a transcription of the stock functions:
    upstream-port/tests/mtk_disp_slot_host_check.c, 37 cases, 0 mismatches (output saved to
    report/mtk-disp-slot-check.txt). It pins the return conventions (write answers with the value it was
    given, not a status, per cmdq_helper_ext.c:2202; a read that finds nothing yields 0 with rc 0), the
    index scaling by 4, the count bound PAGE_SIZE/4, and two outcomes of the range-based lookup: an index
    past the end of every pool is dropped, and if a neighbouring pool starts there the access lands in it -
    stock behaviour, kept rather than quietly improved. The one intentional difference is the lookup offset's
    type: long here where stock uses s32, identical for pools within 2 GiB (the only arrangement this board
    produces) and safer beyond it, with the divergence demonstrated by case [6] rather than argued.

Semantics kept rather than "fixed", because a landed or future vendor callsite can observe each one: the
range-based match so an out-of-range index is not an error; read returning 0 on an unknown handle; write
returning the value; no barrier around the store (stock has none, and adding dma_wmb() would order writes
the vendor engine does not order).

Deliberately not included: cmdqBackupFreeSlot (stock frees at module exit; this display code never does -
the one landed caller, disp_probe_1() at ddp_drv.c:499, allocates 5 slots once), the
cmdq_alloc_mem/cmdq_cpu_read_mem/cmdq_cpu_write_mem indirection layer, per-client pool selection
(CMDQ_CLT_DISP is the only client), and stock's debug logging, current->pid recording, prefetch and CPR
handling. drivers/misc/mediatek/cmdq/v3 has no Makefile in this tree, so its .c files are inert source kept
for the include closure: no symbol here collides with them, and the nm check above confirms it.

Gating: drivers/soc/mediatek/Makefile keys the object on CONFIG_MTK_DISP_BRINGUP rather than on a new
symbol, so there is one switch and no way to build the display objects without their provider. A symbol of
its own was tried first and rejected on measurement: default MTK_DISP_BRINGUP did not follow an existing
.config that had already recorded n, and select could turn it on but never back off. The same arrangement
(one file gated on a symbol declared elsewhere) already exists two lines below in that Makefile, where
mtk-cmdq-helper.o is gated on CONFIG_MTK_CMDQ_MBOX from drivers/mailbox/Kconfig.

Reassessment of the GCE requirement, from these callsites: cmdqRecWrite's 29 references are reachable
inside the landed set only through ddp_manager.o, which forwards a struct cmdqRecStruct * argument it
receives from its callers, and nothing in the landed set creates a recorder handle at all (0 references to
cmdqRecCreate). So the record write is a link-time requirement, not a runnable one, until the DSI/config
layer lands. That is the boundary the next slice has to respect: no #mbox-cells, no compatible, no
port-local provider, until stock evidence says which is right.

No functional claim: the display path is not exercised on a device. disp_probe_1() will allocate and seed
five slots and disp_get_ovl_bandwidth() will read them back from CPU-visible memory, but with 502
unresolved references the display core still cannot be built into a usable kernel, and nothing is flashed or
booted.
---
 drivers/misc/mediatek/video/Kconfig           |   6 +-
 drivers/soc/mediatek/Makefile                 |   5 +
 drivers/soc/mediatek/mtk-cmdq-disp-slot.c     | 239 ++++++++++++++++++
 .../linux/soc/mediatek/mtk-cmdq-disp-slot.h   |  32 +++
 4 files changed, 281 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)
 create mode 100644 drivers/soc/mediatek/mtk-cmdq-disp-slot.c
 create mode 100644 include/linux/soc/mediatek/mtk-cmdq-disp-slot.h

